xt":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in "},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"degeneracy d."}],"kind":"symbol","title":"MaximalCliques","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques":{"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques","url":"\/documentation\/cliques","abstract":[],"title":"Cliques","role":"collection","kind":"symbol"}}}